#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f1becc-876c-5da6-91ba-a413daa8081c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358238} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9d49ee-3e76-5ed8-bcfd-151781e0bce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358709} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb07c96-81ef-5fff-bbec-0f14455ab5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359135} {:answer/id #uuid "b001451c-b518-5952-9037-ae3298169cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359279} {:answer/id #uuid "beae462a-d2b9-5b73-8054-445289e4e01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb07c96-81ef-5fff-bbec-0f14455ab5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359135} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a73fb3-c55f-5cfb-9539-d9135bd7d9a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa6bb928-4008-5011-93e0-eb9181dde1e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94741" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of functions of the form $p(x)=ax,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).$\n\nII. Multiplication by $1$ does not change elements in $U.$\n\nIII. For any $p(x)\\in U$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in U$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162008 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6289ad34-2393-5f5f-b7f0-a1ab1a3916f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Quadratic Form Types Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb4cb75-80df-5e9e-8e15-9b2deaf15df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357990} {:answer/id #uuid "822234f1-654e-5b25-9714-19e56e8c61c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358891} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1b77c7-bae9-5d79-8b90-01000fdf719b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358997} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ce6222-f361-5ea2-9a9b-e005ac93837b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359309} {:answer/id #uuid "e81c3981-0bfd-5f24-9e91-45fa0d0c0d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bb4cb75-80df-5e9e-8e15-9b2deaf15df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357990} :answer-field/id #uuid "e99e818e-f6e2-5daf-9430-475b47a72fb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08e6755d-cc77-5492-aef8-539543b12cc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94744" :question/problem "Which of the following statements regarding quadratic forms are true?\n\n- $R(x)=8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ is positive-definite\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x$ is negative-definite\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Recall that, a quadratic form $Q(x)$ is:\n\n- positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- negative-definite if $Q(x)<0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0};$\n\n- indefinite if \n\n- $Q({x}_{1})>0$ for some ${x}_{1}\\in {R}^{n},$ and\n\n- $Q({x}_{2})<0$ for some ${x}_{2}\\in {R}^{n}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting ${x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ${x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & =8\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic form is indefinite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-2{x}_{1}^{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =-(2{x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2})<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$ So, the quadratic form is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0},$ the quadratic form is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfbf9b7-a07e-542b-a597-9750c31b2725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357992} {:answer/id #uuid "2da1825d-25ca-5c11-ae3d-ffe9a907d4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358161} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb5682b-c9c3-56a3-b594-0f9799c9399f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358560} {:answer/id #uuid "84d526b6-d199-5f7d-af62-0c8a4566dcb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358919} {:answer/id #uuid "ef67d861-3251-5724-8600-a2005c81b738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef67d861-3251-5724-8600-a2005c81b738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359884} :answer-field/id #uuid "aea0550b-1d20-5eca-81bf-e793ca69d02e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0b4abe9a-af7a-5741-a378-7c07564b4cd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94776" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that $\\{{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is linearly independent and ${v}_{1}=2{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}+{v}_{4},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}$\n\nII. ${x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has only zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0$\n\nIII. $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly independent set" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, solving equation ${v}_{1}=2{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}+{v}_{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{4}.\n$$\n\nThis means that ${v}_{2}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{3},$ and ${v}_{4}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\{{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is linearly independent, we have that\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas only zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution, the set $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is a linearly dependent by definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb17d35-beba-56b7-b518-cdee0ad49d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358547} {:answer/id #uuid "6400ebaf-8db1-56e0-b99b-6bb546ecfdb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358627} {:answer/id #uuid "748bdd8c-3bfe-54bd-8357-28c29d87c8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358761} {:answer/id #uuid "b23a2b9b-c75c-50e2-8fd5-a03253a6f335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359303} {:answer/id #uuid "da6316e0-3e8d-5e2c-8c87-4642d8810d84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b23a2b9b-c75c-50e2-8fd5-a03253a6f335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359303} :answer-field/id #uuid "651186fa-d9c3-5fd2-aa9d-2e51ddee4326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cbf9c53e-84bd-5e5c-95e0-1c3b39f57ad9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94779" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{1}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}{v}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){v}_{1}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}+0\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163ffad0-80a6-539d-8e6b-64374abd1df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357946} {:answer/id #uuid "19d9bcf8-e4a4-52f9-ac4a-111a71d24ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357979} {:answer/id #uuid "8db7e5b2-3d4e-5297-896d-239fb1e98258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358985} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ee6723-3e23-5c92-85e3-93d38611c29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359328} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c18f8-2e2b-527a-8f2c-1fa1098563c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc8c18f8-2e2b-527a-8f2c-1fa1098563c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359391} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4d7423e-3ee3-5c28-b3f1-0e8d6605b5f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "db8c1e1e-2555-50dd-bb83-7ef5667f1f43" :question/math-academy-id "q-94781" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{3}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${2{v}_{1},{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+1\\cdot ({v}_{3}-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}({v}_{3}-{v}_{1})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3},{v}_{3}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(3{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+(3{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{2{v}_{1},{v}_{2},3{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement III is false.  If ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0$ has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}+0\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4105f1cb-eb48-5946-92f7-cd5e67689912" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type by Calculating the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109cedc3-19aa-5207-b48c-96f795ee7f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357888} {:answer/id #uuid "10b997f5-5dd0-5130-8cdd-265aa8d61c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357890} {:answer/id #uuid "28f9a9e9-a956-56fb-a443-52eb64708784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358126} {:answer/id #uuid "3e668324-67f2-58d4-9e59-813e5312ef3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358285} {:answer/id #uuid "75cdee28-4d93-5a9f-a720-1f3bc794b111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e668324-67f2-58d4-9e59-813e5312ef3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358285} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a5e346-b891-587b-97d5-0b1d81d4beb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13d1d5cf-031d-5ebf-8e4c-d550f344afdd" :question/math-academy-id "q-94795" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is indefinite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038f75a1-36a7-5c4b-880b-bdbcf5ecb868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357790} {:answer/id #uuid "15b89224-21e3-5afa-b892-82043685a7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357944} {:answer/id #uuid "42a1c9c8-246a-5d10-8a72-e72d492d8316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358319} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3860f1-2c3c-5e04-ada7-39990c0a2725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359234} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf53933-cef3-5406-b909-0c77bb201113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac3860f1-2c3c-5e04-ada7-39990c0a2725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359234} :answer-field/id #uuid "e96d17b6-72d2-5378-b571-48d9f7f1a935" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81d008a7-fd35-5fff-9f9d-fc0e8e60bfec" :question/math-academy-id "q-94797" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-3$ and $det(A)=2$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-3$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-3<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a29e059-d65f-5d62-b01c-37b18136c460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357980} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee25ec4-bcb2-5291-be57-885cdb79f62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358433} {:answer/id #uuid "dadee0ab-1ae1-5ba7-8edb-4687f5cbd5be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359667} {:answer/id #uuid "fa3c9f7c-baa2-55db-bd2c-bf3d202afac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359963} {:answer/id #uuid "fd411a6d-6380-518c-add2-05e9da2d1aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa3c9f7c-baa2-55db-bd2c-bf3d202afac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359963} :answer-field/id #uuid "b265e44d-162b-5f3f-936c-1f7baba37eb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95ed5c64-873a-5214-9b7f-9e356847bad2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94801" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)=17$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=17>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b713b3d-fc6c-590a-b1e5-1d0810686cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357848} {:answer/id #uuid "29903524-d4a4-57a6-b822-c51ffd68ce6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358132} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd2aeb3-4945-54a1-a741-df7c771c1746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358296} {:answer/id #uuid "d015158d-8218-5ed2-88e2-52616061852a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359574} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cae56a-bc1c-58d4-87d5-1dd1b0e73834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fd2aeb3-4945-54a1-a741-df7c771c1746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358296} :answer-field/id #uuid "8982ada9-1ecf-5b2b-9819-ea2bac24fb9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43115d1d-8694-5a95-b413-4600fbd2bc68" :question/math-academy-id "q-94804" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-5$ and $det(A)>0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-5$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-8<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b8ffd00-7d83-5c6f-a43e-f0726e64f0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358143} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9e26f2-1b44-5494-9c55-fca3f8436dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358391} {:answer/id #uuid "982526f5-f07b-57ed-8112-7671aa9e55bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359085} {:answer/id #uuid "debef593-cd34-5a74-8505-112b9bda8363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359709} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a37496-5e05-5898-b625-e0ef07b88eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "debef593-cd34-5a74-8505-112b9bda8363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359709} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1797410-c3bd-5ccf-b7de-0991be959ead" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7d5f731a-8eca-5400-88d7-dd10fc541120" :question/math-academy-id "q-94805" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=8$ and $det(A)<0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=8$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=8>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=21>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbebdd1b-0716-55cb-8df6-12a35b517ab5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Form Type Using Sylvester's Criterion"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68309de4-18f7-5889-be48-f00e7506f433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358664} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb4972b-2f15-5309-b5e6-dc443bbae8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358703} {:answer/id #uuid "806a0787-5de9-5da6-b273-814038c73c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358873} {:answer/id #uuid "8216c5b2-a468-5968-a315-301d5a318927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358889} {:answer/id #uuid "bd38c07a-1cba-5b1f-8e8c-a2e9675cfae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cb4972b-2f15-5309-b5e6-dc443bbae8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358703} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b034084-01c8-5694-bb0b-5922115b26dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0bcde67-e6f1-5859-87e0-ff664264d2ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-94806" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-6$ and $det(A)<0$\n- ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- $A$ is negative-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-6<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-6<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68aac3c2-1b25-59ae-aadf-d9cd9f13fadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358669} {:answer/id #uuid "77567355-0dfd-5191-9862-c90831b7f295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358779} {:answer/id #uuid "858a10b2-1b7b-5eef-9a00-b7d1cd62095c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358924} {:answer/id #uuid "93c431da-4ce2-5fc4-9ac1-12c5b8b2a54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359046} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c3e619-ac9f-5922-96ba-2d9ac9a3d724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93c431da-4ce2-5fc4-9ac1-12c5b8b2a54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359046} :answer-field/id #uuid "d593dbcb-b46f-55d2-a1d7-38424638c175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac8aaff6-9636-552f-aae2-acac5b76a9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-94842" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${t,{t}^{2}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- ${t}^{2}+1$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${t,{t}^{2}}$\n- ${t,{t}^{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot t+{x}_{2}\\cdot {t}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that all linear combinations of\n\n$$\n{t,{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nresult in polynomials without the constant term.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, the polynomial ${t}^{2}+1$ can't be written as a linear combination of the set  ${t,{t}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a63bf8-e5e8-54ad-bb88-edd2237429e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358120} {:answer/id #uuid "786bf61e-0e29-57bb-bc96-4839523f35c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358790} {:answer/id #uuid "79a47bfd-e059-5523-a011-a586ae1cdb6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358808} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3beacd-722d-57d9-85fc-b711ee71b402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358971} {:answer/id #uuid "bf571ce9-b0dd-5125-8fc5-c2e00b46b815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf571ce9-b0dd-5125-8fc5-c2e00b46b815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359426} :answer-field/id #uuid "093025e1-26ce-5fd4-bc74-e37175f6bed8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8792da5-8116-548d-a9e9-a07944635528" :question/math-academy-id "q-94847" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $3{t}^{2}-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$\n- ${0,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3{t}^{2}-t=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot t+3\\cdot {t}^{2}+0\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07bbb53a-4ed2-50b5-bc22-2929108a1e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357820} {:answer/id #uuid "3ded4180-a55a-5901-a539-8002ecba961b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358282} {:answer/id #uuid "58d026f6-65f7-5278-af81-bfcdea120f96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358523} {:answer/id #uuid "ad245027-ebb5-5634-a773-a7afaac07aef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359248} {:answer/id #uuid "ba39c8af-4b9d-5c10-97b1-262e297dcafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07bbb53a-4ed2-50b5-bc22-2929108a1e88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357820} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc36438-bfe6-5475-ac9c-649c82bce831" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b873442b-9d2d-5d41-a7b6-d67882fe4b7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94848" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- ${t}^{3}-3t+8$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$\n- ${1,t,{t}^{2},{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2}+{x}_{4}\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{t}^{3}-3t+8=8\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot t+0\\cdot {t}^{2}+1\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any polynomial in ${R}_{3}[t],$ we get \n\n$$\na{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct+d=d\\cdot 1+c\\cdot t+b\\cdot {t}^{2}+a\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c3388a-c053-5f60-b2b3-e56e51b6b670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357957} {:answer/id #uuid "28e1a0ea-1e90-56e2-897f-dbc9f53fa5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358125} {:answer/id #uuid "44d858c4-5e84-5c9c-90c4-dae727fd9adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358340} {:answer/id #uuid "82192a20-c511-5aaf-b5bc-6549769f0294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358890} {:answer/id #uuid "de814b57-fa42-5fb3-a87c-833458976b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17c3388a-c053-5f60-b2b3-e56e51b6b670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357957} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5fc346a-7206-5add-8203-46927a460ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf9e33e0-00b4-5d7d-a1a7-55573563c2a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94850" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,{t}^{3}}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{3}[t]$\n- $-{t}^{3}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,{t}^{3}}$\n- ${0,{t}^{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}_{3}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {t}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n-{t}^{3}=0\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot {t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f37d4e-f946-5858-b746-1322fc40e999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358071} {:answer/id #uuid "45982a79-2f0f-57e8-ad37-19abed6441d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358346} {:answer/id #uuid "642164d3-ec79-5b13-b89f-3543f6b222a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358628} {:answer/id #uuid "93699939-728e-5c2c-80e8-3af88146b906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359041} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaec45c-67da-5511-b4ab-a5d33a92d321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93699939-728e-5c2c-80e8-3af88146b906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359041} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4210f0e-1e0b-573b-8873-efdd52a770a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0064f075-2267-5e41-8556-443f519b3150" :question/math-academy-id "q-94859" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{12}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}]}
